&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 1&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50186960</guid><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:12:28 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Ask HN: How do you handle AI security for automated vulnerability management?</title><link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50186510</link><description>&lt;p&gt;Hello World!&lt;p&gt;So I am new to the world of cybersecurity, and after some back and forth with AI, I chose to use a specific tool that was recommended for automated vulnerability detection &amp; patching, but I think the issue I encountered is something common to all similar platforms that provide AI security fixes in 2026.&lt;p&gt;It proposed a fix to an issue labeled 'critical'. I gave the specific file and the fix to Gemini, and, it disagreed strongly.&lt;p&gt;My trust in the platform immediately degraded.&lt;p&gt;It shows in my thinking that AI security platforms for repositories are unreliable, unless you cross check every critical/high vulnerability as a human with another AI tool as well. For example, for another issue labeled 'High', the fix, I cross-checked, was correct in that case.&lt;p&gt;But that that one 'critical' issue, which turned out to be not a bug at all, and certainly not critical at all, has degraded my trust strongly in AI security platforms for automated vulnerability detection &amp; patching. Note, to prevent confusion, with automated patching I mean automated pull requests with AI-proposed fixes, pending explicit human merging.&lt;p&gt;Now, I wonder what you guys use (instead, if at all), and how reliable your security tool(s) are, if at all, for automated vulnerability detection (&amp; patching.)&lt;p&gt;Happy Coding!&lt;p&gt;Worldwide Wishes,&lt;p&gt;Behan&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
